# Standard UUID generation surface (B03).
#
# Design: generating a UUID draws operating-system entropy, and a
# version 7 UUID also reads the clock, so the generators are TOOLS,
# never builtins, for the reason std/random.cor and std/time.cor give:
# tool calls are traced and substituted under replay, so a program that
# generated a UUID generates the same one again on every re-run, and the
# checker refuses tool calls inside `@deterministic` bodies.
#
# Parsing is pure, so it is the builtin String method
# `text.parse_uuid()`, which needs no import. A UUID is a String in the
# canonical form: 36 lowercase characters, hyphenated 8-4-4-4-12.
# See docs/reference/text-and-encoding.md.

public effect uuid_generate:
    reversible: true

# A random UUID (RFC 9562 version 4): 122 random bits.
public tool uuid_v4() -> String uses uuid_generate

# A time-ordered UUID (RFC 9562 version 7): the Unix time in
# milliseconds, then 74 random bits. Later UUIDs sort after earlier
# ones, which keeps database indexes compact. Two UUIDs generated in the
# same millisecond are ordered randomly.
public tool uuid_v7() -> String uses uuid_generate
